      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "@id": "https://www.stselectronicrecyclinginc.com/billings-mt#faqpage",
      "mainEntity": [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does STS provide electronic recycling services in Billings, Montana?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "STS Electronic Recycling provides scheduled electronic recycling services for Billings organizations and the wider Yellowstone County area. Projects can include computers, laptops, servers, monitors, network equipment, phones, printers, and other retired business technology. STS reviews the equipment list, collection requirements, access needs, and documentation goals before scheduling. Pickup availability depends on volume, equipment type, timing, and project scope. Billings businesses can contact STS to discuss a practical collection and processing plan."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How does Billings electronics recycling support data security?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "STS supports Billings data-security programs through documented chain of custody, NAID AAA certified destruction for applicable media, and R2v3 certified electronics processing. NIST SP 800-88 provides media-sanitization guidance for selecting clear, purge, or destroy methods based on device type and data sensitivity. Billings organizations can request asset documentation and Certificates of Destruction for applicable projects. These records help support internal information-security policies, vendor oversight, and audit preparation."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is pickup available for electronics recycling in Billings and Yellowstone County?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Scheduled pickup may be available for qualifying electronics recycling volumes in Billings and Yellowstone County. STS reviews the equipment mix, estimated quantity, pickup location, loading access, and requested documentation before confirming service. Organizations with computers, servers, displays, network hardware, or larger office technology refreshes can request a project assessment. Pickup terms depend on the specific Billings project, so STS recommends reviewing scope before collection is scheduled."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What certifications are used for Billings electronic recycling projects?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "STS uses R2v3 certified processing for electronics recycling and NAID AAA certified destruction for data-bearing media. R2v3 certification addresses responsible recycling, downstream tracking, environmental management, and data-security controls within the processing system. NAID AAA certification applies to data destruction services. Billings and Yellowstone County organizations can request documentation appropriate to their equipment and media-handling requirements. NIST SP 800-88 is used as a media-sanitization standard, not as a certification."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How quickly can a Billings electronics recycling pickup be scheduled?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Many Billings organizations can discuss same-week scheduling after STS reviews the equipment list and project scope. Actual timing depends on collection volume, equipment type, building access, loading requirements, and whether serialized tracking or data destruction is needed. Yellowstone County projects with complex logistics may require additional coordination. Contacting STS early in a technology refresh, office move, closure, or equipment replacement project helps establish a collection plan that aligns with operational timing."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can Billings healthcare and public organizations use STS for retired technology?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "STS works with Billings healthcare, education, government, and business organizations that need documented handling for retired technology. Healthcare projects may require PHI chain-of-custody controls and documentation that supports HIPAA compliance programs. Public organizations and schools may need asset records aligned with procurement or technology-refresh processes. STS reviews the equipment type, data-bearing devices, project schedule, and reporting requirements before proposing a Yellowstone County collection and processing approach."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What happens after electronics are collected from a Billings organization?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "After collection from a Billings organization, equipment is managed through documented transport, sorting, reuse evaluation, data destruction when requested, and R2v3 certified processing. Functional equipment may be evaluated for reuse or remarketing, while materials designated for recycling are processed through responsible downstream channels. Data-bearing media can receive NAID AAA certified destruction for applicable projects. Billings and Yellowstone County clients can receive applicable recycling reports and Certificates of Destruction to support their internal records."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can Billings businesses recover value from retired IT equipment?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Some Billings businesses may have qualifying technology with reuse or remarketing potential. STS can review equipment age, condition, configuration, quantity, and marketability as part of the project assessment. Asset recovery is not available for every device or collection, and recycling remains the appropriate path for obsolete, damaged, or non-marketable equipment. Yellowstone County organizations should provide an accurate inventory when possible so STS can help determine whether reuse evaluation fits the planned IT asset disposition project."
          }
        }
      ]
